913:
The Little Towaliga River is classified as a small water supply watershed. A small water supply watershed is one that contains less than one hundred (100) square miles of land within the drainage basin upstream of a governmentally owned public drinking water supply intake. Eady Creek Reservoir contains nineteen and eight-tenths (19.8) square miles of drainage area upstream of the City of Barnesville raw water intake and the Little Towaliga River watershed contains forty-nine and nine-tenths (49.9) square miles drainage area upstream of the low head dam intake. The Eady Creek Reservoir watershed is contained within the Little Towaliga River watershed.
Stream corridor criteria.
(1) The perennial stream corridors of a small water supply watershed within a seven-mile radius upstream of a governmentally owned public drinking water supply intake or water supply reservoir are protected by the following criteria:
a. A buffer shall be maintained for a distance of one hundred (100) feet on both sides of the stream as measured from the stream banks;
b. No impervious surface shall be constructed within a 150-foot setback area on both sides of the stream as measured from the stream banks;
c. Septic tanks and septic tank drain fields are prohibited in the setback area of b. above.
(2) The perennial stream corridors within a small water supply watershed but beyond a seven-mile radius distance upstream of a governmentally owned public drinking water supply intake or water supply reservoir are protected by the following criteria:
a. A buffer shall be maintained for a distance of fifty (50) feet on both sides of the stream as measured from the stream banks;
b. No impervious surface shall be constructed within a 75-foot setback area on both sides of the stream as measured from the stream banks;
c. Septic tanks and septic tanks drain fields are prohibited in the setback areas of b. above.
(3) The following criteria apply at all locations in a small water supply watershed:
a. No sanitary landfills are allowed in the Eady Creek water supply watershed drainage area;
b. New hazardous waste treatment or disposal facilities are prohibited;
c. The impervious surface area, including all public and private structures, utilities, or facilities, of the entire water supply watershed shall be limited to twenty-five (25) percent, or existing use, whichever is greater. Determination of compliance of this section regarding impervious surface area shall be made by the Lamar County Zoning Administrator by comparison of proposed development of impervious area versus open spaces to the total current impervious area and open space of the reservoir drainage area. The total impervious area ratio shall be calculated at time of adoption of this article and maintained in the office of the Lamar County Zoning Administrator;
d. All permitted and acceptable land disturbing activities will be vegetated or re-vegetated immediately with appropriate grasses or legumes;
e. Agricultural activity including farm ponds shall be consistent with best management practices established by the Georgia Department of Agriculture, and shall comply with Georgia Pesticide Act of 1976, Georgia Pesticide and Use Act of 1976, and Georgia Laws 1982, House Bill 1780 (O.C.G.A. § 2-1-4);
f. Forestry management within the forty-nine and nine-tenths (49.9) square mile drainage area will follow the Georgia State Forestry Commission's best management practice guidelines for streamside management zones, stream crossings, access roads and their construction, timber harvesting, site preparation, and reforestation and forest protection (prescribed burning, firelines, and chemical fire retardant);
g. New livestock feedlot operations will be allowed provided best management practices are followed;
h. New industrial development projects will be allowed on a case-by-case basis. Industrial facilities which handle hazardous materials of the types and amounts determined by the Department of Natural Resources shall perform their operations on impermeable surfaces having spill and leak collection systems as prescribed by the Department of Natural Resources;
i. Permitting for activities in the watershed will be governed by the watershed protection criteria of this section 2-25-23, administered by the Lamar County Planning Commission through the zoning administrator. It shall be the responsibility of every land user, developer or builder to present plans for land use alterations within the drainage areas to the Lamar County Planning Commission for review and approval. Plans for commercial projects shall bear a stamp by a Georgia registered engineer and shall include at a minimum the calculated total impervious area(s) and shall have a detention/retention pond to control stormwater runoff from the site.
(Ord. of 6-21-99, § V)
